# Settings for scripts/run-stages-1-3.sh, run-stages-4-6.sh and drive.sh.
# Copy to .env (gitignored) and edit. All three also take the workspace as a
# positional argument, which wins over WORKSPACE for that run.

# Which workspace the stage scripts operate on. A bare name is taken as
# workspaces/<name>; anything containing a "/" is used as the path as written.
WORKSPACE=mosque

# The versioned config both stages 2 and 4-6 are generated against.
CONFIG=config/default.yaml

# --- optional ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The Stage 3 export Stage 4 consumes. Defaults to <workspace>/review.json.
# REVIEW_SUBMISSION=workspaces/mosque/review.json

# Only ever supplied on the command line, so it is read back out of the workspace
# manifest. Set it here to override what the last acquisition recorded.
# DRIVING_SIDE=left

# 1 adds -v to every osm-scenario command.
# VERBOSE=0

# --- scripts/drive.sh only --------------------------------------------------

# The MetaDrive checkout's own interpreter. A different one from this repo's on
# purpose: MetaDrive is 3.8 / numpy 1.24 and this repo is 3.10 / numpy 2.2.
# METADRIVE_PYTHON=/home/keith/Desktop/work/wingfin/metadrive/.venv/bin/python

# Which card renders: auto (default) uses the discrete GPU when the machine has
# one, integrated forces the built-in graphics, nvidia insists. It cannot be a
# flag on drive.py -- the GLX loader settles it before python starts.
# GPU=auto

# Painted lane-line width in metres. MetaDrive's own thickness is in pixels, so
# its real width changes with the size of the map; this asks in metres instead.
# drive.py's own default (0.15, about a real road marking) applies when unset,
# and a --line-width-m after -- still wins over this. 0 uses MetaDrive's 2 px.
# LINE_WIDTH_M=0.15

# How finely a painted line is sampled before it is drawn, in metres. MetaDrive's
# own 2.0 lays 2 m chords inside every curve, so the tarmac shows past its own
# line. drive.py's default is 0.25. Anything under 1.5 also draws broken lines
# 3 m / 3 m instead of 2 m / 2 m, which is MetaDrive's own STRIPE_LENGTH; 2.0
# puts both back.
# LINE_INTERVAL_M=0.25

# How many times a second the simulator advances, for drive.sh and
# sensor-survey.sh. MetaDrive's own 10 applies when unset, and that is also the
# only rate this repo's converter writes unless it is told otherwise. Set from
# the two keys env.step multiplies together, so 100 gives a 0.01 s physics tick
# with one substep; 10 gives exactly MetaDrive's (0.02, 5), so STEP_HZ=10 and
# leaving it unset are the same run.
#
# The surprise worth knowing: a dataset can only be *replayed* at the rate it was
# converted at. ReplayEgoCarPolicy consumes one recorded frame per env.step with
# no interpolation, and a baked light tape is indexed the same way, so drive.py
# refuses a mismatch rather than driving the route at the wrong speed.
#
# So each rate gets its own directory -- `convert` with no flag writes
# <workspace>/scenarionet-10hz and `--step-hz 100` writes scenarionet-100hz --
# and one workspace can hold several. This variable picks *which one* the two
# scripts drive as well as the rate they run at, so STEP_HZ=100 drives
# scenarionet-100hz. A --step-hz after -- wins over it, for both.
#
# Converting is deliberately NOT wired to this variable -- `convert --step-hz` is
# typed out, because a dataset's rate is baked into bytes the Stage 3 review never
# re-checks and picking it up from a machine-local file is how two workspaces end
# up at different rates with nobody having decided.
# STEP_HZ=10

# How many times a second the policy is consulted and the --sensors are read, for
# drive.sh. The middle rate of world tick / decision + camera / physics, and the
# one MetaDrive has no clock for: env.step is the world tick, the policy call and
# the camera draw all at once, and the sensor config has no slot for a rate. So
# this is a stride counted in our own loop, and it must divide STEP_HZ.
#
# Unset it is the step rate, which is what every run before this flag existed had.
#
# STEP_HZ=100 with DECISION_HZ=20 is 100/20/100: the world and the physics at
# 100 Hz, a decision every fifth step. That is what openpilot's bridge is written
# for -- its _DT_MDL is 0.05 s -- and it beats converting a 20 Hz dataset for the
# purpose, the control interval being the same 0.05 s with ten times the physics
# underneath.
#
# On --agent-policy replay it gates the sensor read alone: MetaDrive drives the
# ego from the tape in-engine on every step, so there is no decision there to
# decimate. On idm, manual and remote the action is held across the steps between.
# DECISION_HZ=20
#
# And step-timing.sh reads neither of the two above: it drives *every* rate the
# workspace holds, each at the rate it was written at, because picking one would be
# the opposite of the comparison it exists to make. Its own --decision-hz goes
# after the -- , where it applies to every dataset at once.

# Names the machine in every row of a step-timing CSV, and in the file name. The
# hostname otherwise -- which inside a container is a random id, so name it there.
# A --label after -- wins over this.
# STEP_TIMING_LABEL=laptop

# --- scripts/step-timing-docker.sh and compose.yaml only ---------------------

# Only for a camera-rig spec kept OUTSIDE the repo. The ones in rigs/ need
# nothing here: the repo is mounted at /work and worked from, so
# `--camera-rig rigs/cams.txt` is the same string inside the container and out.
# Set this and the directory is mounted read-only at /rig, reachable as
# `--camera-rig /rig/<name>.txt`. Falls back to docker/rig/, which exists so an
# unset RIG_DIR cannot make docker create a root-owned directory in $HOME.
# RIG_DIR=/some/where

# METADRIVE_PYTHON above must stay commented for the container to work.
# _common.sh sources this file with `set -a`, so an uncommented host path here
# would override the interpreter the image sets and the sweep would die on a
# missing venv. The image points it at its own, where MetaDrive is installed
# alongside the converter on 3.10 rather than in a separate 3.8 venv.
